[Music]  one of the topics that we think is  really interesting is what happens when  CEOs take a very controversial but  personal position on topics related to  the environment and our society or even  political and it gets a lot of attention  and you know customers and employees see  this and it can have some either  positive or negative consequences on the  company we're finding it's about yeah  maybe ten percent or even less of the  CEOs are engaging in activism in one  form or another so it's not like it's  everybody doing it but the ones that are  doing it tend to be very well known very  popular  you know sometimes founders sometimes  people that just have had a visionary  impact on the corporation  the types of things we're interested in  or activism by the CEO along things that  reflect their personal view we're not  talking about positions that the CEO  takes that you could imagine the  position being taken is one that makes  the the company better off something  like immigration visas for engineers you  can imagine companies that are tech  companies that's a really good thing and  they may be controversial but it's  really something that would be helped  them as a company so one thing that's  interesting based on our surveys is  there's certain topics that society  seems to want people away on and other  other topics not so topics like health  care clean energy environment seem to be  very very popular but things like  abortion religion politics things like  that they tend to want the CEOs to stay  out of that so something like I think we  all agree is really important to talk  about is gun control you know it's a  super controversial area especially  depending on what part of the country  you're from and so you know do you want  to take a take a stance or potentially  fund some initiative on gun control well  you know you're gonna have a lot of  employees and customers that'll find  that to be a good thing and others that  we're finding out to be something you  gotta really stay out of  so CEOs are much more visible there's a  lot more eyes on them so when CEOs are  activists so they take a very personal  position on a controversial topic it  shows up almost immediately there's a  velocity of social media such that you  could go from nothing to something  pretty serious in a matter of you know  minutes or hours and you know that can  explode either in good ways or bad ways  if you take a position that most  customers don't agree with I mean you  could lose sales or if you take a  position that's not consistent with what  employees want you can you could have a  hard time attracting and retaining  employees one of the things we're doing  now is taking a look at what happens to  sales and purchases of companies  typically in a retail space but where  CEOs taken a very strong position on a  controversial topic you know did the  sales go up or down things like that if  you take a stance on something you know  you could start you know doing some  arithmetic where you know what customers  might we lose or what customers might we  gain by doing these things same thing on  the employee side you know there are  some employees and be turned off by this  and maybe leave and others would be  attracted to it the flip side of it is  that we've done some surveys which find  is that about you know two-thirds of the  people want to CEOs take a leadership  position somebody's controversial  environmental social issues it varies a  bit depending on your political  affiliation or you know what generation  you're in whether you're a Boomer Gen X  or millennial or whatever but the costs  and benefits are something you want to  think about you know it's hard to  quantify but it may be the case that  cameras are willing to you know maybe  take a hit in terms of the economics but  actually do something you know really  good for the environment or other  stakeholders one thing we find in the in  the research is some early indications  that consumers are willing to buy more  from companies that take that activist  position on positions that they agree  with we find the flip side as well that  they're likely to buy less from  companies that take activist position on  something that they disagree with so I  mean these positions can you know shift  you know customer purchases in all the  way you know it affects the bottom line  of the company  the one thing I think is really key is  that the board in the government  structure of the organization have to  anticipate these things and they want to  talk about it ahead of time and not be  blindsided and I think the smart boards  are actually kind of going through a bit  of scenarios where what would we do if I  think the boards really need to think  about do they want the CEO you know  making personal statements about these  things or not and you know what pushback  might be well it's the CEO saying and  it's his personal opinion or her  personal opinion but you know really  it's hard to separate the CEO of the  company as a person from the company  itself so that could either be a good  thing or a bad thing you want to put  kind of constraints on them you know  kind of lay that out in a formal way and  then if you take these positions you  know you might want to think about  what's the cost of benefits of doing  that for shareholders so it's it's  complicated  so the role the board is is tricky and  it's evolving especially now companies  have historically been in the u.s. at  least focused on shareholder value more  recently some of the big institutional  investor is like Larry Fink at Blackrock  have sent letters out to boards saying  hey you have to take these other  stakeholders into account customers  employees the society environment and  even the government so I mean there's  push to sort of change from a strict  shareholder focus and that kind of  setting you can imagine CEOs advocating  certain positions that the view is  better for society and kind of embrace  the stakeholder views where it's like  let's really start paying serious  attention to the environment or  workplace diversity or things like that  so I think it's gonna be interesting to  see how the boards focus changes and I  think it's gonna be interesting to see  how aisle CEOs respond but it's a it's a  it's an evolution  you can imagine at some point people  demanding you know here are these topics  that we feel strongly about as customers  and employees what is your stance you  know on this kind of this kind of thing  there's a lot of things that are would  probably make you know the environment  better off that are very costly you know  where you know the the value to  shareholders is probably not positive  that's that's where it becomes very very  complicated are you willing to make  those investments because just the right  thing to do for for those stakeholders  and I think that whole thing has to be  mapped out it's certainly a lot more  discussion about stakeholder interest  now you know people investing in  environmental social and governance  sorts of funds you know more money  pouring into that you know that type of  thing you can have a whole new  generation of board members that are  that you know sort of understand this  stuff grew up with it and I think it's  going to change the discussion they'll  be interested to see how that plays out  going forward  you  [Music]

